  "worked_solution": "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$\n\nis the first Frobenius solution, and the second Frobenius solution is determined as follows:\n\n- **Case 1**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\notin Z,$ then \n${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{2}){x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 2**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2},$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\n- **Case 3**: If ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}\\in N,$ then\n${y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+{x}^{{\\lambda }_{2}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is ${\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\ge -2={\\lambda }_{2}.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nNow, since ${\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}=2-(-2)=4$ *is* a positive integer, the other solution is given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={b}_{-1}{y}_{1}(x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+{x}^{-2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere the constants ${b}_{n}$ are found by substitution.",
